# AGENTS.md

Guidance for coding agents (and humans) working in this repository. Read it
before making changes. It intentionally repeats the basics: agents arrive with
no memory of previous sessions, and a rule that is only implied is a rule that
gets broken.

## Setup

Clone, then run `npm install`. Node 22 is the supported runtime; the build uses
the TypeScript compiler configured in `tsconfig.json`. The service is a small
edge worker: it starts, registers shutdown handlers, and exits cleanly when the
platform asks it to. There is no database and no background queue.

## Commands

- `npm run build` — type-check and emit to `dist/`.
- `npm test` — run the unit suite once. Use this before declaring work done.
- `node dist/index.js` — start the service locally.

Never add a watch mode to CI. Never run the built service with production
environment variables on a developer machine.

## Code conventions

- TypeScript strict mode is on; do not weaken `tsconfig.json` to make an error
  disappear. Fix the error instead.
- Prefer explicit types at module boundaries (`export`ed functions) and let the
  compiler infer locals.
- Errors are handled at the edge: catch, log with context, and keep serving.
  A handler must never leave a promise unobserved.
- Shutdown handlers must be idempotent and fast; the platform gives the process
  a short window and then kills it.
- No new dependencies without a comment explaining what they replace and why
  the platform's built-ins are not enough.

## Testing expectations

Every change to `src/` comes with or updates a test that fails without it.
Tests live next to the code as `*.test.ts`. Do not delete or skip a failing
test to get a green run; that is a reportable incident, not a fix. If a test is
wrong, fix the test and say so in the PR description.

## PR etiquette

- One logical change per PR. Drive-by renames are not welcome.
- The PR description states: what changed, why, how it was tested, and how to
  revert it.
- Keep diffs small. A PR that touches more than a dozen files needs a plan in
  the description or it will be sent back.
- Do not force-push over a branch someone else has reviewed; add commits and
  let review follow the trail.

## Communication

When you are unsure between two approaches, stop and ask in the PR draft
instead of guessing and defending the guess later. When a reviewer asks for a
change you disagree with, implement it or make the counter-argument with
evidence; never silently ignore a review comment. Report anything that looked
wrong but was out of scope so a human can file it.

## Escalation

Stop and escalate to a human when: a change would alter authentication,
authorization, or secret handling; a dependency introduces native builds; a
migration of stored data would be needed; or CI fails for a reason you cannot
explain in one sentence. These are not agent decisions.

## Notes on history

This file grew out of several incidents where an agent optimised locally and
broke globally: a shutdown handler that threw synchronously and skipped the
rest, a test deleted because it was "flaky" (it was not), and a dependency
added to avoid reading platform docs. Each rule above traces to a real
failure. When a rule blocks legitimate work, change the rule in a PR of its
own rather than working around it quietly.

## Reminders

Re-read the conventions section after long sessions; context drift is the most
common source of avoidable mistakes. Prefer boring, obvious code over clever
code. The service is small on purpose; keep it that way. When in doubt, write
the comment you wish you had found. Delete dead code in the same PR that made
it dead. Do not leave TODOs without an owner. Ask early, not after a wrong
implementation has been reviewed. Measure before claiming a performance win.
Keep logs greppable. Treat every new dependency as a permanent guest. The
build stays deterministic: no network access at build time. Local development
stays offline-first. The repository stays lint-clean at every commit, not just
at release time.

## Working with the platform

The edge platform cold-starts processes often, so module-level work must stay
cheap: no reading large files at import time, no opening sockets, no spawning
threads that outlive a request. Configuration arrives through environment
variables with stable names; parsing happens once in a dedicated module and
everything else consumes the parsed result. If a configuration value is
missing, fail fast with a message that names the variable. Never guess a
default for a security-relevant setting.

Time and randomness come from injected abstractions, never from direct calls,
so tests stay deterministic. Clock skew between the platform and your machine
is real: never compare timestamps across that boundary without a stated
tolerance. Retries use jittered backoff and a bounded budget; a retry loop
without a budget is a bug.

## Reviewing agent work

Humans reviewing agent-authored PRs in this repository should read the diff
before the description: the description states intent, the diff states fact.
Watch for the classic failure modes — tests adjusted to fit broken behaviour,
errors swallowed to make a loop terminate, types loosened one `any` at a time,
and helper functions extracted for reuse that nothing reuses. Ask for the
revert plan explicitly; if the author cannot produce one, the change is not
ready.

Agents reviewing human work apply the same standards in reverse. Do not
approve a change because the author is senior; approve it because the diff is
correct. When you cannot understand a change well enough to defend it, say
that plainly instead of approving with a hedge.

## Glossary

- Edge worker: a short-lived process the platform starts near traffic and
  disposes of. Assume any process can disappear between two lines.
- Shutdown handler: a callback registered at import time and run once on
  SIGTERM. Idempotent, fast, ordered.
- Budget (policy): the shared byte allowance this file and other policy files
  draw from. Long files get truncated, so brevity here is a feature, not a
  style preference.

## Ownership

The `src/` tree is owned collectively; nothing in it requires a specific
person's sign-off beyond normal review. The platform configuration files are
owned by the on-call rotation: propose a change, do not merge it unreviewed.
When ownership is unclear, the default is one review from whoever knows the
area best, found by `git log`, not by asking in a channel and waiting.
